.TH VCONSOLE 3
.SH NAME
vconsole \- native virtio-serial guest management stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind '#Y0' /mnt/vconsole
.B cat /mnt/vconsole/status
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The vconsole device connects a modern-capability PCI virtio-console/serial
controller to an eve-only binary stream. It accepts exactly the nonconsole
port named
.BR org.qemu.guest_agent.0 .
It is currently specific to the QEMU Guest Agent port, not a general
arbitrary-port or terminal driver.
.PP
The PC64 kernel must include the driver and boot with
.BR qgavirtio=1 .
Exactly one supported console controller must be present, with no competing
driver. Endpoint publication is asynchronous after the worker starts.
With no published endpoint, attach fails rather than waiting for hardware.
.PP
Attach specifications are empty (slot zero), 0, 1, 2 or 3. The normal boot
supervisor uses slot zero. Slots are endpoint identities, not arbitrary port IDs.
Each directory contains:
.TF status
.TP
.B data
Exclusive read/write binary stream, mode 0600. Only ORDWR is accepted.
Reads and writes may block and return short counts; offsets have no meaning.
The driver preserves NUL and other nontext bytes and has no UART ctl file.
.TP
.B status
Read-only bounded text, mode 0400. It reports numeric identity, epoch,
owner-generation, owner/stopped/claimed/handle-held state and request phase
counts, irq-vector and irq-deliveries. Vector -1 means no checked route;
deliveries counts recognized ISR activity on the checked vector and saturates
at the unsigned 64-bit maximum. It contains no payload or DMA addresses and is not a hardware
health attestation.
.PP
The directory is mode 0500. Attach, walk, stat, open, read, write and block-read
check eve identity, including operations through delegated descriptors.
Close is allowed for native clunk cleanup. Create, remove, wstat, truncation
and remove-on-close are denied. Native dup shares the same refcounted Chan;
the final close retires exactly that guest owner.
.PP
Host socket disconnect/reconnect preserves the guest owner, receive state and
handles; a positive write waits for host reopening. This is distinct from
guest final close, port removal or terminal fault, which require complete
reset and drained references before release. Blind retry after cancellation,
short writes or timeout can duplicate side effects; these are not rollback
guarantees.
.PP
INTx is deliberately masked by default. The worker polls at 100 millisecond
deadlines. An experimental checked-API kernel build may opt in using
.BR qgavirtioirq=1 ;
it unmasks only after checked registration and DRIVER_OK. Registration and
polling do not establish interrupt delivery; require matching-vector ISR
activity and separate fault/reset validation on the target platform.
Four fresh contexts are boot-pinned and never recycled. Exhaustion or failed
drainage can require reboot. Retry counts are not hard scheduling deadlines.
.SH FILES
.TF #Y0/status
.TP
.B #Y0/data
Normal QGA binary stream.
.TP
.B #Y0/status
CPU-side endpoint snapshot.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/port/devvconsole.c
and
.B /sys/src/9/port/vconsole*.[ch]
with PC adapters in
.B /sys/src/9/pc/vconsole*-pc.c
and
.BR /sys/src/9/pc/vconsole-wake.c .
The standalone repository exports this layout; installation is explicit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dup (2),
.IR intro (3),
.IR qga9 (8)
.SH BUGS
Experimental, modern PCI/PC64 only. Basic local QEMU IRQ, forced reset and
named-port recovery tests do not establish failed-reset behavior, PCI-controller
hot-unplug or complete kernel DMA/cache/lifetime safety.
Filesystem permissions do not isolate eve from eve.
The stream neither authenticates QGA nor establishes host isolation.
